    "text": "A refrigerator keeps food cold by moving heat from inside to outside, like carrying warmth away in a bucket. A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes. Inside the fridge, the refrigerant absorbs heat from your food and turns into a gas. Then a compressor squeezes that gas, making it hot. That heat travels through coils on the back or bottom of the fridge and escapes into your kitchen. Next, the refrigerant cools down and turns back into a liquid, ready to soak up more heat. This cycle repeats over and over. Because heat keeps getting removed, the inside stays cold, keeping your food fresh."
